\noindent{\bfseries Abstract.}
There exists two different types of top-down object detection methods based on appearance models with no prior constraints, such as saliency or texture information, and methods using semantic attributes like edge detection and region extraction. As shown in \cite{Park2017Differential} these classes of method detect most objects without problems by simply considering a combination of several input frames; however when occlusion occurs they fail due to their intrinsic shortcomings related both externally (e.g., different viewpoint) or internally (overly sensitive boundaries). To address these issues we propose combining saliency prediction via machine learning and graph-cut optimization. Specifically, we focus on an object detector that learns not just edge detection but also other types of feature extraction for better segmentation performance while simultaneously being able to recover missing/incomplete features using our new technique called differential clustering. Experimental results demonstrate significant improvements over state-of-the-art algorithms in terms of accuracy rate achieved during testing even under challenging conditions including high variations within objects themselves along with unpredictable backgrounds .\\
The goal of our approach is not only improving previous work concerning its computation time complexity but also providing useful visualizations which help users understand underlying mechanisms driving data acquisition process through application of human expertise into computer vision pipelines involving deep neural networks. This allows us to effectively solve real world tasks as long as necessary preprocessing steps have been properly addressed so as to make sure input images conform required properties such as cleanliness , color variation etc,... Finally thanks again to everyone who helped write this document including supervisor(s), project advisers &amp; co-authors .
