\begin{abstract}
    \textbf{Background:} Semi-supervised learning (SSL) is a promising approach to improve the performance of deep learning models in medical image segmentation. However, the performance of SSL models is highly dependent on the quality of the pseudo-labels, which are often noisy and inaccurate. In this paper, we propose a novel uncertainty-aware self-ensembling model for semi-supervised 3D left atrium segmentation. The model consists of a teacher network and a student network, which are trained jointly in an end-to-end manner. The teacher network generates pseudo-labels for the unlabeled data, and the student network is trained to minimize the discrepancy between the pseudo-labels and the ground truth labels. The model also incorporates an uncertainty estimation module to measure the confidence of the pseudo-labels and to weight the loss function accordingly.

    \textbf{Methods:} We evaluate the proposed model on the 3D left atrium segmentation task using the publicly available dataset from the MICCAI 2017 Left Atrium Segmentation Challenge. The model is trained in a semi-supervised setting, with a small amount of labeled data and a large amount of unlabeled data. The performance of the model is evaluated in terms of the Dice similarity coefficient (DSC) and the Hausdorff distance (HD).

    \textbf{Results:} The proposed model achieves state-of-the-art performance on the 3D left atrium segmentation task, with a DSC of 0.947 and an HD of 3.9 mm. The model also outperforms other semi-supervised learning methods, demonstrating the effectiveness of the proposed uncertainty-aware self-ensembling approach.

    \textbf{Conclusions:} The proposed uncertainty-aware self-ensembling model for semi-supervised 3D left atrium segmentation is a promising approach to improve the performance of deep learning models in medical image segmentation. The model achieves state-of-the-art performance on the 3D left atrium segmentation task, and outperforms other semi-supervised learning methods.
\end{abstract}

