\begin{quote}
\textbf{Abstract:}
\textit{
In this paper, we propose a novel Siamese representation learning framework for abdominal multi-organ segmentation. The proposed framework consists of two branches, each of which is a 3D U-Net. The two branches share the same encoder and decoder, but have different decoders. The two branches are trained in a Siamese manner, where the two decoders are trained to predict the same segmentation mask. The two branches are trained to minimize the difference between the two predicted segmentation masks. The proposed framework is trained end-to-end. The proposed framework is evaluated on the abdominal multi-organ segmentation task. The proposed framework is compared with the state-of-the-art methods. The proposed framework achieves the best performance.
}
\end{quote}

\subsection{Thesis}

The following is the full text of the abstract for a thesis titled "A Study of the Effect of the Use of a Siamese Network on the Performance of a Convolutional Neural Network" from the University of Texas at Austin:

\begin{quote}
\textbf{Abstract:}
\textit{
In this study, we investigate the effect of the use of a Siamese network on the performance of a convolutional neural network. We use a Siamese network to train a convolutional neural network. The Siamese network consists of two convolutional neural networks. The two convolutional neural networks are trained to predict the same output. The two convolutional neural networks are trained to minimize the difference between the two predicted outputs. The convolutional neural network is trained to minimize the difference between the predicted output and the ground truth output. The convolutional neural network is trained end-to-end. The convolutional neural network is evaluated on the MNIST dataset. The convolutional neural network is compared with the state-of-the-art methods. The convolutional neural network achieves the best performance.
}
\end{quote}

